    "https://jobs.okstate.edu/jobs/postdoctoral-scholar-bf9457-stillwater-oklahoma-united-states": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scholar BF9457",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Scholar BF9457 - Stillwater, Oklahoma, United States Postdoctoral Scholar BF9457 - Stillwater, Oklahoma, United States Kiosk mode Postdoctoral Scholar BF9457 Apply Now Postdoctoral Scholar BF9457 495084 Stillwater DIV AGRICULTRL SCI & NATURAL RESRCS (STW) Adjunct/Temp Faculty PhD DIVISION OF AGRICULTURAL SCIENCES AND NATURAL RESOURCES Department of Biosystems and Agricultural Engineering 215 A Agricultural Hall Stillwater, OK 74078 o I 405-744-5431 bae.okstate.edu Post Doctoral Scholar – Evapotranspiration Forecasting and Irrigation Decision Support The Department of Biosystems and Agricultural Engineering is seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Scholar to lead the Oklahoma component of the project, which centers on turning NASA Earth observations into forecasts that irrigators and water agencies can act on. Groundwater from the Ogallala–High Plains Aquifer supports millions of irrigated acres across Kansas, Oklahoma, and Texas, and its continued decline is among the most consequential water sustainability challenges in the United States. The project delivers 10-day forecasts of crop evapotranspiration and irrigation demand, with modeling indicating potential water savings of 10–15% without a yield penalty. Position Description This position is a post-doctorate scholar position that will develop and evaluate a baseline crop evapotranspiration (ETc) forecasting capability that integrates OpenET historical and near-real-time ET retrievals with GraphCast weather predictions, targeting a 10-day forecast horizon under non-limiting water conditions. Enhance ET forecasts through soil moisture assimilation, using NISAR L-band backscatter with SMAP and Sentinel-1 as complementary datasets, and machine learning approaches that downscale coarse soil moisture products to field-relevant resolution across heterogeneous irrigated fields. Translate forecasted crop water demand into crop-stage-aware irrigation demand estimates, incorporating dynamic crop coefficients, effective rooting depth, growing degree days, and satellite vegetation indices for the region’s dominant irrigated crops — corn, soybean, cotton, sorghum, and alfalfa. Evaluate operational feasibility within producer-facing advisory platforms, including the Oklahoma Agricultural Scientific Irrigation Scheduling (OASIS) tool under deployment at OSU, and support prototype deployment with the Oklahoma Water Resources Board and the Oklahoma Mesonet. Build and maintain reproducible data pipelines, including automated OpenET API access, NISAR and SMAP retrieval, and Oklahoma Mesonet ingestion; perform quality assurance on eddy covariance ET, soil moisture, and producer irrigation records; and prepare metadata and derived products for archiving in NASA-approved repositories under the project’s Open Science and Data Management Plan. Coordinate seasonal field campaigns at OSU irrigation research sites and cooperating producer fields across Oklahoma, and validate forecasts against eddy covariance towers, Mesonet observations, and producer irrigation records. Disseminate results through peer-reviewed publications, technical reports, and presentations at regional and international meetings, and contribute to stakeholder workshops and proposal development. Qualifications Ph.D. in Biosystems or Agricultural Engineering, Civil or Water Resources Engineering, Environmental Engineering, Hydrology, Agronomy or Soil Science, Remote Sensing, Geography, Data Science, or a closely related field, completed by the appointment start date. Demonstrated research productivity evidenced by first-authored peer-reviewed publications. Proficiency in scientific programming and experience working with large geospatial or time-series datasets. Python is strongly preferred, as existing project workflows are Python-based; strong proficiency in R, MATLAB, or Julia, with the ability to work in Python, will also be considered. Research experience in evapotranspiration estimation, soil water balance modeling, hydrologic or crop water modeling, or satellite remote sensing of agricultural water use. Strong written and oral communication skills in English, and the ability to work both independently and as part of a multi-institution team. Valid driver’s license and willingness to travel to field research sites across Oklahoma. Employment Conditions Review of applications will begin 20 days after job is posted. For full consideration, submit all materials as soon as possible. Review of applications will continue until a suitable candidate is identified. Salary and benefits are competitive and commensurate with education, training, and experience. Retirement and long-term disability, medical, dental, and life insurance programs are available to the employee. The position is available as soon as an outstanding candidate is available. OKLAHOMA COOPERATIVE EXTENSION SERVICE • OKLAHOMA AGRICULTURAL EXPERIMENT STATION FERGUSON COLLEGE OF AGRICULTURE About the Department Biosystems and Agricultural Engineering is a unique department shared between two colleges, Ferguson College of Agriculture and the College of Engineering, Architecture and Technology. The department enhances the production and profitability of agricultural and biological products, and the conservation and management of natural resources through teaching, research, and outreach. We have graduate engineering (PhD, MS) and undergraduate (BS, accredited Biosystems Engineering, BS Agricultural Systems Technology) programs. We take pride in being an inclusive department in which students from any background can feel comfortable, supported and successful. For more information about the department visit our website at https://bae.okstate.edu/ , and check us out on our socials: Facebook: @baeokstate, X: @BAEOkstate, and Instagram: @baeokstate. Application To apply, candidates should submit: (1) a cover letter (less than 2 pages) describing research experience and fit with the position, (2) a current CV, (3) contact information for three references, and (4) one representative publication, code repository, or technical writing sample. Questions may be directed to Dr. Sumon Datta at sumon.datta@okstate.edu with the subject line \"NASA Postdoctoral Scholar — ET Forecasting”. Formal applications must be submitted electronically to https://jobs.okstate.edu To learn more about OSU Agriculture and Stillwater, visit these websites: https://agriculture.okstate.edu https://agresearch.okstate.edu https://extension.okstate.edu https://www.okstate.edu https://www.visitstillwater.org https://www.stillwaterchamber.org All positions are subject to the availability of funding, and any offers are contingent upon the successful completion of a background check. Federal law requires employers to provide reasonable accommodation to qualified individuals with disabilities. Please tell us if you require a reasonable accommodation in the application process. Examples of reasonable accommodation include making a change to the application process, providing documents in an alternate format, using a sign language interpreter, or using specialized equipment. To request an accommodation, please email eeo@okstate.edu or call 405-744-5449. Oklahoma State University, as an equal opportunity employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding non-discrimination. Oklahoma State University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all individuals and does not discriminate based on race, religion, age, sex, color, national origin, marital status, disability, or veteran status with regard to employment, educational programs and activities, and/or admissions. For more information, visit eeo.okstate.edu. Sign Up for job alerts Job Alert f8ccbf21 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories Faculty/Instructor Faculty/Instructor Employment Type Adjunct/Temp Faculty Adjunct/Temp Faculty Locations Stillwater, Oklahoma, United States Stillwater, Oklahoma, United States Posting Campus OSU-Stillwater OSU-Stillwater Minimum Education Level PhD PhD First Name (required) 43d4554d Last Name (required) b740b418 Email (required) 0e37cbaa Send Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting this form, you consent to receive text messages related to available jobs and information about employment within the OSU/A&M System. Message & data rates may apply. Message frequency varies. Unsubscribe at any time by replying STOP or clicking the unsubscribe link (where available). By submitting your information, you acknowledge that you have read and agree to all system campus privacy policies. Thank you Save this job for later First Name (required) ffa406bc Last Name (required) 40b08ada Email (required) 947b63f3 Save this job for later 2dca625c Send Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting this form, you consent to receive text messages related to available jobs and information about employment within the OSU/A&M System. Message & data rates may apply. Message frequency varies. Unsubscribe at any time by replying STOP or clicking the unsubscribe link (where available). By submitting your information, you acknowledge that you have read and agree to all system campus privacy policies. Thank you You may also be interested in Postdoctoral Fellow BF9460 Stillwater DIV AGRICULTRL SCI & NATURAL RESRCS (STW) Post Doctoral Fellow of Barley Breeding and Genetics BF9456 Stillwater DIV AGRICULTRL SCI & NATURAL RESRCS (STW) Research Specialist II - Senior Research Specialist BS4977 Stillwater DIV AGRICULTRL SCI & NATURAL RESRCS (STW) Apply Now! First Name (required) 0ba27351 Last Name (required) 270d6a98 Email (required) fb71996e Apply Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting this form, you consent to receive text messages related to available jobs and information about 