    "https://jobs.okstate.edu/jobs/assistant-or-associate-professor-213709-stillwater-oklahoma-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor 213709",
      "description": "Assistant or Associate Professor 213709 - Stillwater, Oklahoma, United States Assistant or Associate Professor 213709 - Stillwater, Oklahoma, United States Kiosk mode Assistant or Associate Professor 213709 Apply Now Assistant or Associate Professor 213709 495030 Stillwater COLLEGE OF VETERINARY MEDICINE (STW) Faculty Other professional degree Campus OSU-Stillwater Contact Name & Email Gayle Sisney, gayle.sisney@okstate.edu Work Schedule Monday through Friday, 8:00am-5:00pm with occasional evenings and weekends. Appointment Length 11 months Hiring Range Commensurate with education and experience Special Instructions to Applicants For full consideration, please include a resume, cover letter and contact information for three professional references. About this Position Faculty Position in Veterinary Epidemiology, Preventive Medicine, and Public Health The Department of Veterinary Pathobiology in the College of Veterinary Medicine at Oklahoma State University invites applications for a full-time tenure or career track faculty position at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor in veterinary epidemiology, preventive medicine, and public health . Rank and appointment will be commensurate with the candidate's qualifications and experience. This position offers an exciting opportunity to join a collaborative faculty dedicated to excellence in teaching, service, and scholarship while advancing preventive medicine, herd health, epidemiology, and One Health through partnerships with Oklahoma's state and federal animal health agencies, beef cattle and food animal industries, practicing veterinarians, and producers. Qualifications Required qualifications include: D.V.M. (or equivalent veterinary degree) from an AVMA-accredited institution. M.S. in Epidemiology, Preventive Medicine, or a Master of Public Health (MPH). Preferred qualifications include: Ph.D. in epidemiology Experience or advanced training in herd preventive health management, particularly in rural and underserved communities. Board certification in the American College of Veterinary Internal Medicine (Large Animal), the American Board of Veterinary Practitioners (Beef Cattle or Dairy Practice), or equivalent specialty certification. Diplomate status or eligibility for Diplomate status in the American College of Veterinary Preventive Medicine-Epi (ACVPM). Primary Responsibilities The successful candidate will be expected to: Teach veterinary epidemiology, preventive medicine, and related subjects in the professional veterinary curriculum. Participate in activities of the Center for Rural Veterinary Medicine. Provide consultation and expertise in herd health management, disease prevention, and outbreak investigations. Contribute to graduate education through mentoring and participation in the College's graduate curriculum. Participate in departmental, college, and university service activities. Required Qualifications D.V.M. D.V.M. (or equivalent veterinary degree) from an AVMA-accredited institution. M.S. in Epidemiology, Preventive Medicine, or a Master of Public Health (MPH). (degree must be conferred on or before agreed upon start date) Preferred Qualifications Other Professional Degree Ph.D. in epidemiology Certifications, Registrations, and/or Licenses: Board certification in the American College of Veterinary Internal Medicine (Large Animal), the American Board of Veterinary Practitioners (Beef Cattle or Dairy Practice), or equivalent specialty certification. Diplomate status or eligibility for Diplomate status in the American College of Veterinary Preventive Medicine-Epi (ACVPM). Skills, Proficiencies, and/or Knowledge: Experience or advanced training in herd preventive health management, particularly in rural and underserved communities. All positions are subject to the availability of funding, and any offers are contingent upon the successful completion of a background check. Federal law requires employers to provide reasonable accommodation to qualified individuals with disabilities. Please tell us if you require a reasonable accommodation in the application process. Examples of reasonable accommodation include making a change to the application process, providing documents in an alternate format, using a sign language interpreter, or using specialized equipment. To request an accommodation, please email eeo@okstate.edu or call 405-744-5449. Oklahoma State University, as an equal opportunity employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding non-discrimination. Oklahoma State University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all individuals and does not discriminate based on race, religion, age, sex, color, national origin, marital status, disability, or veteran status with regard to employment, educational programs and activities, and/or admissions. For more information, visit eeo.okstate.edu. Sign Up for job alerts Job Alert 0b53a814 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories Faculty/Instructor Faculty/Instructor Employment Type Faculty Faculty Locations Stillwater, Oklahoma, United States Stillwater, Oklahoma, United States Posting Campus OSU-Stillwater OSU-Stillwater Minimum Education Level Other professional degree Other professional degree First Name (required) 1c74fd50 Last Name (required) a9a1e585 Email (required) 16a23e8f Send Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting this form, you consent to receive text messages related to available jobs and information about employment within the OSU/A&M System. Message & data rates may apply. Message frequency varies. Unsubscribe at any time by replying STOP or clicking the unsubscribe link (where available). By submitting your information, you acknowledge that you have read and agree to all system campus privacy policies